WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.420
Open a virtual machine in SUSE Observability and follow its relations to its host, storage, and network in the Topology perspective.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.080
Open the main menu.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.500
Expand SUSE Virtualization.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.550
Select Virtual machines.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:28.430
The view lists the VMs of every connected cluster. Open the VM named kasm, which runs on the far-edge cluster.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:39.200
Highlights opens first. The About card shows the VM's status, host, IP address, vCPUs, memory, and guest operating system.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:48.990
Related resources lists what the VM uses, such as its VM network, its disks, the Longhorn volume, and the virt-launcher pod.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:53.510
Select Topology to draw these relations as a map.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:01:03.490
The VM sits in the Selection row. Its outgoing relations point to its VM network, its host, and its persistent volume claim.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:11.810
Each component is colored by its health, so a problem in the storage or on the host shows next to the VM.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:18.110
A VM that Rancher creates for a guest cluster is also a Kubernetes node, so its map shows the pods inside.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:26.426
Each virtual machine is a component with relations to its host, storage, and network. Next, you investigate an unhealthy virtual machine.
